<h2>How to Choose</h2>
<p><strong>For courses and corporate learning:</strong> Synthesia. Compliance certifications, SCORM export and 140+ languages are the differentiators, and the credit conversion is published so the budget is predictable.</p>
<p><strong>For creator content and marketing:</strong> HeyGen. Lower cost per finished minute, 175+ languages, and a free tier that lets you evaluate the avatar quality properly before paying.</p>
<p><strong>For script-to-video with real footage:</strong> Pictory if you want stock b-roll doing most of the visual work, InVideo AI if you want to keep editing manually afterwards.</p>
<p><strong>For volume on a budget:</strong> Fliki — 0.5 credits per minute is the cheapest published rate in this group, with the trade-off being a smaller avatar library.</p>
<p><strong>The universal check:</strong> multiply the plan's credits by the vendor's credits-per-minute figure before subscribing. That number, not the sticker price, decides whether a plan lasts a week or a month.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>What is the best AI avatar video generator?</h3>
<p>Synthesia for training, courses and faceless channels — 125+ avatars on Starter, 140+ languages, SOC 2 and ISO 27001, Starter at {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.price} for roughly 12 minutes. HeyGen for creator and marketing video at $29/month for 600 credits and 175+ languages, with a usable free tier.</p>

<h3>How much does an AI avatar video cost per minute?</h3>
<p>Synthesia converts at about 100 credits per finished minute: {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.starter.price} buys about 12 minutes and {SYNTHESIA_PRICING.pro.price} about 60, roughly $1.50/minute. HeyGen burns about 20 credits per minute on its newest avatars, so $29 buys roughly 30 minutes — under $1/minute.</p>

<h3>Can I make avatar videos for free?</h3>
<p>Yes, within limits. Synthesia Basic gives about 10 watermarked minutes a month; HeyGen gives three watermarked one-minute videos; Fliki gives 3 credits a month at 720p with a watermark. Enough to judge quality, not enough to publish weekly.</p>

<h3>Do AI avatars look real enough for YouTube?</h3>
<p>For presenter-style explainers, tutorials and course lessons, yes for most audiences. The visible limits are that avatars stay in a presenter frame rather than moving through a scene, and gestures can read as canned in close-ups. Formats that need handheld or cinematic movement are a poor fit.</p>

<h3>Stock avatar or custom avatar?</h3>
<p>A stock avatar is a ready-made presenter from the library; a custom avatar is a clone of a real person built from footage, which keeps a channel visually consistent. Synthesia includes five personal avatars from Pro upwards, Pictory gates custom avatars to Professional, and HeyGen includes one custom video avatar even on the free tier.</p>

<h2>How We Evaluate</h2>
<p>Tools are compared on published pricing, vendor-published credit conversions and documented capabilities. We do not present lab-style test results we did not produce.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> Avatar-led presenter video — training, courses, marketing and faceless creator content.</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> Official pricing pages and documentation, checked on the date shown above; credit conversions taken from the vendor where published.</li>
<li><strong>Criteria:</strong> Cost per finished minute, avatar and language coverage, watermark and export limits, custom-avatar gating, and whether the plan's credits survive a weekly schedule.</li>
<li><strong>Corrections:</strong> Email admin@aizest.net with a source if a figure here is out of date.</li>
